# Introduction

## About 白

**shiro** is an alt keyboard layout targeting English and Spanish (optimized for English prioritarily).

This is a layout that's very non-standard. It re-thinks basic concepts, to the point where it needs its own analyzer for proper stats evaluation.

Its most notable features are the following:

## Default finger positions

The default finger positions aren't `ASDF-JKL;` (using en-us QWERTY as key positions reference), but instead `AWEF-JIO;`.

<!-- (image) -->

This accomplishes a couple of things:

1. The wrists have an easier time resting at the natural angle of the arms, instead of being in a carpal tunnel-inducing straight angle.

2. The finger positions simulate a "vertical stagger"-esque effect: The middle/ring fingers stop being so aggressively arched, and instead rest by default on the upper-row keys, while the index/pinky rest on the home-row keys.

Altering the default positions of the fingers outside of the home-row isn't typically done in other alt layouts, due to the complications that it introduces in the vertical travel of fingers. However, this layout solves this issue by introducing its own **non-standard fingering**.

## Non-standard fingering

Shiro makes it so that no finger ever has to extend upwards from its default position. This means that no finger travels more than a single key vertically.

Under this idea, the occupied keys would be the following:

<!-- (image) -->

However, 

Shiro doesn't treat non-standard/alternate fingerings as patches or workarounds, but as powerful mechanisms with lots of potential for exploitation, through which basically all of this layout's design revolves around.

**On non-standard fingering:** The longer middle/ring fingers resting on the upper keys take care of laterally-adjacent keys (QW-OP ring, ERT-YUI middle), while the index takes care of the GB-HN cluster and the lower CV-M keys. The pinkies are then left with `A\<Z-;./`, never being forced to extend upwards, and having the hand partially or fully have to move with it. 

<!-- (image) -->

This allows for less awkward vertical travel, due to the nonsensical horizontal stagger that standard keyboards have, by replacing it with horizontal travel. It also makes for a very interesting synergy with the index/middle fingers, allowing for huge possibilities in bigram/trigram roll optimization (more on this later).

Meanwhile, **about alternate fingering:** This is where fingering is systematized, and I think it deserves its own section.

## Alternate fingering

### Potential and rationale

Most layouts typically try to avoid alt-fingering, but Shiro completely embraces it.

Back when I was learning and practicing [Gallium v2](https://github.com/GalileoBlues/Gallium), I noticed how I found MORE comfort in some aspects of QWERTY, while I felt certain awkwardness in Gallium, at least on a standard keyboard.

Gallium, and other alt keyboard layouts, are very comfortable to use. But I noticed that, on my regular typing with QWERTY, I tend to abuse non-standard and alt-fingering a LOT. This makes up for a huge amount of my comfort and speed in QWERTY, even if it's intentionally poorly designed on ergonomics. This makes up for lots of wrist and finger movement, which can lead to more strain than necessary.

HOWEVER, I don't believe that completely rejecting fingerings other than the standard is the way to go, since this introduces awkward or restricted motions, especially at higher speeds, that go against the comfort of typing.

At first, I thought that balancing both aspects in fingering would be the best, but after exploring and settling on the non-standard fingering I described above, I realized there was lots of potential if I also heavily involved alt-fingering based on, and dynamically adaptive of **conditions, rules,** and my own personal set of **preferences**.

### Conditions, rules and preferences

The most key aspect of Shiro is that alternate fingering doesn't always follow the same one-off pattern, but instead relies on **context**; alt-fingering intuitively adapts to what's written based on the bigram/trigram to be typed, naturally mitigating SFBs/SFTs while maximizing bigram/trigram rolls.

Through this set of conditions/rules/preferences, alt-fingering allows for an extensively bigger amount of flexibility and comfort/stat-maximizing.

---

**Conditions:**

- **The bigram has to be on the list.** Alts are enumerated per key group, not blanket: the ERT/YUI middle finger rolls into the index for `ER/RE/RT/TR` (and friends through `IJ`, `OK`), into the ring for `UI/IU/IO/OI`, and so on down the board. If the pair isn't listed, you're typing it standard.
- **The trigram has to survive it.** An alternation is only taken if the *third* key doesn't land back on the same finger — `ER` wants to become a MIDDLE–RING roll, but `ERF` would just trade one same-finger pair for another, so the roll extends outward instead. (Rule Y, detailed below.)
- **Same-finger conversion beats everything else.** When a pair *is* a same-finger bigram under standard fingering, the listed alt is what you reach for — it's the whole reason `QS/SQ`, `WD/DW`, `NM/MN` and the rest of the list exist.
- **Low-priority keys flip the default.** On the avoid-keys (`X`, `,`, `.`, …) standard fingering is the fallback you escape *from*: an index alt is preferred as soon as it creates no SFB/SFT, and the low-priority condition is only partially lifted when it does.

---

**Rules:**

- **Standard fingering is the default state**, unchanged priority, unless one of the listed conditions applies (rule T).
- **Bigram choices depend on the surrounding trigram** (rule Y): whenever an alternation would create a same-finger bigram against the third key, extend the roll one finger outward to absorb it — `RTG`, `IUH`, `OIJ` and the whole family follow from one principle.
- **No long same-finger travel through a single letter** (rule Z): one key of leap is fine, two is a problem, especially when the middle letter is already part of a roll with no time to recover.
- **Scissors are non-negotiable by class** (details in [C](#c-finger-scissors)): middle–ring and ring–pinky scissors are never allowed in any direction; index–middle full scissors are only banned in the reverse direction (index extended / middle retracted).

---

**Preferences:**

- **Stronger fingers first** (rule U): when two rolls are otherwise equal, the one built on index/middle wins over ring/pinky.
- **Less travel wins ties** (rule X) — but only if the shorter path still makes a good roll; travel is never bought at the cost of the roll structure.
- **Pinky economy:** frequent letters should never force a pinky off its home keys (`A`, `;`) or upward into the top row; `Z , . /` get the least important letters and punctuation, and `QW/OP` stay with the ring finger precisely so the pinkies never have to reach up there.
- **Context over habits:** where my comfort from years of QWERTY alt-abuse and the stats disagree, the trigram evidence decides — I keep the personal feel as a tie-breaker, not the vote.

---

## Punctuation, space and the rest of the keys

The bottom-right corners keep their usual punctuation (`, . /`), but `;` moves to the **Q key**, the top-left corner. That's the key trade of the whole layout: the right pinky gets to keep `s` on its home key to itself (the most-used consonant never sharing a finger with the most-used punctuation), and `s;` — the commonest punctuation bigram after `.;` and `, ` in prose and code — becomes a quick cross-hand alternation instead of a pinky stutter. At 0.13% of keystrokes, the corner is a perfectly fine home for the semicolon.

The apostrophe gets the **ISO-102 key** (the extra key under Enter on ISO boards), entirely outside the 30-key grid, so `'s`, `s'`, `n't` all fall into the pinky's natural rolls without polluting the letter layout; on ANSI boards, the `'` key right of `;` works the same way. Space goes to the **right thumb** — left and right measured essentially tied (49.5% vs 50.5% into/out of space), so convention wins. Full rationale, including the alternatives I evaluated, is in [`results/LAYOUT.md`](results/LAYOUT.md).

## Spanish (and why "English first" isn't "English only")

English-first describes what the *search optimized*, not a wall. When I scored this layout under a Spanish corpus, it came out on top of the entire reference field there too (5.48 vs Sturdy's 6.38, QWERTY's 10.11) — the vowel-heavy, roll-friendly core transfers better than I expected.

For actual bilingual daily use, there are two dedicated **side layouts** on the English↔Spanish frontier, both keeping the exact same 30 keys so nothing is relearned: `hybrid-35` gives up 0.12 English points for a 17% Spanish improvement, and `hybrid-80` goes further for Spanish-dominant typing. Same grid, same rules, same fingering — just a different letter arrangement you can switch to. Both beat every community layout on *both* languages. Details and the full frontier table: [`results/LAYOUT.md`](results/LAYOUT.md) and `results/archive/hybrid_pareto.txt`.

(One honest gap: `ñ`, accented vowels and `¿¡` are outside the modeled 30-key alphabet — Spanish input at the OS level still comes from dead keys/remapping, as it always did. This repo optimizes the physical layer only.)

## A note on discipline

Everything claimed here has a condition attached: **you have to actually type it the way it's written.** The arched `AWEF-JIO;` home and the listed alts are not decoration — typed with standard ASDF habits, the same-finger numbers regress back to QWERTY territory, and typed with the alts, they essentially vanish (0.02% under context-aware scoring).

There's a second catch: most community analyzers can't see context-dependent alt-fingering at all (none of the twelve tools I tested model a finger as a *decision*), so they will report fixed-fingering numbers that look mediocre by construction. The stats below are from this repo's own analyzer; the tool-by-tool comparison is in [`findings/analyzer_comparison.md`](findings/analyzer_comparison.md) if you want to verify that claim yourself.

Unlearning `ASDF` is the real cost of entry. The per-letter fingering guide in [`results/LAYOUT.md`](results/LAYOUT.md) is the practice target — once the arch and the alt lists are muscle memory, the rest is just typing.

