## Installing the layout (X11)

```bash
xkb/install.sh
```

The script copies `xkb/mine` to `~/.config/xkb/symbols/mine` and applies
it to the current display. Add it to your window manager's autostart to
persist it.

Note: plain `setxkbmap -I ~/.config/xkb -layout mine` fails on many
systems with *"Error loading new keyboard description"* — setxkbmap's
`-I` replaces the entire system XKB path, which breaks the
`include "us(basic")` in the symbols file. The script works around this
by compiling through `xkbcomp` (whose `-I` adds to the default path)
instead. The `No symbols defined for <...>` notices xkbcomp prints for
exotic evdev keys are normal and harmless.

---

# Design specification

The rules below are the original design constraints the layout was
optimized under.

**Notation.** QWERTY key names are used throughout as *physical position
labels* only — e.g. "E" means "the key in the E position on a standard
ISO English keyboard", never the letter E itself. In finger-group
notation, `/` splits the hands (`ASDF/JKL;` → left of `/` is the left
hand). "ISO key" refers to the extra key in the bottom row on ISO
boards (listed as the last `/` in `ZX/,./`).

## A. Key fingering

1. **Rest (home) position: `AWEF/JIO;`** — left pinky→A, ring→W,
   middle→E, index→F; right index→J, middle→I, ring→O, pinky→;.
   These keys, not `ASDF`/`JKL;`, are the home row the fingers rest on.
2. **`ERT`/`YUI` = middle finger.** `RT` may be alted by the index in a
   MIDDLE–INDEX / INDEX–MIDDLE roll for bigrams `ER`, `RE`, `RT`, `TR`.
   `IO` may be alted by the ring finger (MIDDLE–RING / RING–MIDDLE) for
   bigrams `UI`, `IU`, `IO`, `OI`.
3. **`QW`/`OP` = ring finger.** `W`/`O` may be alted by the middle
   finger, following MIDDLE–RING / RING–MIDDLE, for bigrams
   `QW`/`WQ`, `OP`/`PO`.
4. **`H`** can be pressed by either index; the left is preferred. The
   right index may take it when a roll with a middle-finger key before
   or after is possible.
5. **Defaults:** `A`/`;` pinky, `S`/`L` ring, `D`/`K` middle,
   `FG`/`HJ` index.
6. **`SDFG`/`JK` index alts** — MIDDLE–INDEX / INDEX–MIDDLE rolls — when
   these bigrams occur: `QS`/`SQ`, `WD`/`DW`, `EF`/`FE`, `RG`/`GR`,
   `IJ`/`JI`, `OK`/`KO`. Otherwise use standard fingering. The `QS`/`SQ`
   alt is preferable to the standard-fingering same-finger bigram, but
   should still be avoided if possible: it takes too much wrist and
   finger movement.
7. **`VB`/`NM` = index finger.** `V`/`M` may be alted by the middle
   finger (MIDDLE–INDEX / INDEX–MIDDLE) for bigrams `VB`/`BV`,
   `NM`/`MN`.
8. **`C` = left index**, with a middle-finger alt (MIDDLE–INDEX /
   INDEX–MIDDLE) for bigrams `CV`/`VC`.
9. **`X`/`,` = ring finger**, with a middle-finger alt for bigrams
   `XC`/`CX`, `,M`/`M,`. I dislike the default fingering of these keys:
   they should carry low-priority (least-used) letters. On rolls, the
   low-priority condition does not apply.
10. **`ZX/,./` (last `/` = the ISO key) = pinky fingers.** The pinky
    home keys `A`/`;` are completely fine for frequent letters, but the
    `ZX,./` keys should be avoided where possible, or given the least
    important letters under the same low-priority rule as `X`/`,` .
    `X`, `,`, and `.` may additionally be alted by the index finger (if
    no SFB/SFT is involved); the low-priority condition is then not
    fully lifted, but the index alt is preferred over the standard
    fingering. These five keys are prime candidates for punctuation — a
    hint, not a mandate; final placement is up to evaluation.

## B. Additional rules and preferences

- **T.** All keys using standard fingering hold the same priority,
  unless specified otherwise.
- **U.** Finger rolls involving stronger fingers are preferred over
  rolls involving weaker fingers.
- **X.** Alt-finger rolls with less finger travel are preferable to
  alt-finger rolls with more finger travel. Less travel should always
  be good, as long as it stays balanced with good rolls.
- **Y. Bigram choices depend on the surrounding trigram.** Example: `ER`
  could be alternated as MIDDLE–RING instead of standard MIDDLE–MIDDLE
  (to avoid the same-finger bigram), but on the trigram `ERF` the `R`–`F`
  pair would then press the same finger (index). So `ERF` is alted as
  RING–MIDDLE–INDEX instead. The pattern generalizes to all the
  trigrams of the MIDDLE–RING alternations: `RTG` (left hand), `IUH`
  (right), `OIJ` (right), etc. The bigram lists elsewhere in this spec
  are indicative only: whenever an alternation would create a
  same-finger bigram against the third key, extend the roll one finger
  outward to absorb it. Concretely, on MIDDLE–INDEX N–M bigrams, when
  `O` is introduced the alternation can move over to M–O, leaving N to
  the ring finger.
- **Z. Avoid long same-finger travel through a single letter.** On the
  trigram `VBR`: `VB` is alted as MIDDLE–INDEX, then `R` must be
  pressed by the middle finger right after `B` rolled into the index —
  a rapid middle-finger leap over more than one key with only one letter
  in between, made worse because that letter is part of a roll (even
  less time to move). One key of travel would be fine; more is an
  issue.

## C. Finger scissors

> "A scissor is when a finger extends to the far side of the keyboard at
> the same time as an adjacent longer finger flexes to the near side."
>
> "A scissor bigram is a bigram that creates a scissor motion between
> adjacent fingers. Scissors are classified by distance: half-scissor
> bigram (HSB, 1u between the two keys), full-scissor bigram (FSB, 2u
> or more)."
>
> — [layouts.wiki — Scissors](https://layouts.wiki/reference/metrics/scissors/)

Severity varies by finger pair: index–middle scissors are generally the
most tolerable (higher finger independence), middle–ring moderate, and
ring–pinky the least tolerable.

My tolerances:

- **Index–middle, middle extended / index retracted** (wiki-type): fully
  tolerated, half- or full-scissor.
- **Index–middle, index extended / middle retracted** (reverse): prefer
  to avoid half-scissors; **fully intolerant of full scissors** in this
  direction.
- **All middle–ring and ring–pinky scissors: fully intolerant**, at any
  distance.

## D. Rationale

The heavy reliance on alt-fingering comes from a personal observation.
As a long-time QWERTY user who later learned Gallium v2 in search of a
more comfortable typing experience, I found I had an *easier* time
typing at speed on badly-optimized QWERTY than on Gallium v2 — because
on QWERTY I constantly alt-finger terrible SFBs into seamless, fast,
comfortable rolls, while Gallium v2 barely uses any alt-fingering at
all.

That raised the question this repo answers: what if a layout were
designed *for* alt-fingering from the start — using it as a first-class
resource to allow higher rolls, lower SFBs/SFTs, fewer scissors, and
balanced finger usage? Such a layout would be as close as possible to a
"perfect layout", at least for me.
